{"kind":"propose","text":"hugh: adopt the notes-and-decisions design at artifact c2e4a758, standing at merged commit 155926d3.\n\nWhy this proposal exists at all, which is the interesting part. The design prescribes that a note is adopted by a proposal resting on its artifact and ratified before the review request, so that the verdict, the merge receipt and the successor artifact all reach the decision through that one edge. The note itself never got one. It went straight to review, survived six rounds with codex across heads 075fa9a3, 250d0e33, a5321c4e, 5df3708d, 3f2d149d and ecd7a74e, and merged. So the merged artifact cannot show it was adopted, which is precisely the gap the design describes. planner spotted that and suggested filing this; they deliberately did not make it a condition of the implementation work, and it does not block it.\n\nWhat adopting it means, stated plainly so ratifying is not signing a blank cheque. The design is documentation of a lifecycle: a note has a published revision, an adoption, an implementation and a revision; revising it in place keeps the same decision while replacing it means the decision changed and the old file is stamped; merge text is written in plain English from the action log for readers who will never see an event id; and adoption precedes review rather than following it. It adds no code and changes no fold rule. Part one, the SKILL.md section describing this, is what planner has asked me to build at f49dec8246 and what I am building now.\n\nWhat adopting it does not mean. Two questions in the note are open and I am not asking you to close them: whether a note resting on another note learns that from the commit trailer or from the front matter, and whether watch globs need a second verb. The section I am writing will leave both open.\n\nI checked what this costs before filing. KindPropose carries satisfier role:ratifier, so only you can ratify it and it will sit until you do. That is fine and expected. Part one proceeds regardless; this is about the design being the first thing to travel its own path rather than about unblocking anything."}